{"body":"## Goal\n\nShow upcoming household bin collections in HomeDash so residents can see which bins need to go out and when, including alternating collection weeks.\n\nThis is a feature request and design-research record, not an implementation-ready specification. The defaults below are requested by the owner; the broader international scheduling model is a recommendation pending specification. No code changes have been made.\n\n## Requested default setup\n\nUse the owner's Australia/New Zealand household pattern as a convenient starting preset, not as a universal claim about every council:\n\n| Collection type | Default colour | Default schedule |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| General waste | Red | Weekly on the configured council/suburb collection day |\n| Recycling | Yellow | Every two weeks on that same day, on week A |\n| Garden waste | Green | Every two weeks on that same day, on week B |\n\n- Allow every default colour to be changed independently to match the actual suburb/provider. Colour is presentation, not the identifier of the waste stream or schedule.\n- Ask for the household's collection weekday and one known recycling collection date. Use that local date to anchor week A; garden waste starts one week later.\n- The preview must show general waste plus recycling in week A, then general waste plus garden waste in week B.\n- Allow bins to be renamed, disabled, or added, and schedules to be customised. Do not force the preset on councils with a different service.\n\n## US and European research\n\nReviewed official municipal/provider sources on 10 October 2026. These are representative local examples, not national standards or a comprehensive survey of Europe.\n\n| Location | Documented collection pattern | Design implication |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Seattle, US | Single-family garbage and food/yard waste are weekly; recycling is every other week. | More than one stream can run weekly; recycling and organics need not alternate. |\n| New York City, US | Address-specific schedules; compost is weekly on recycling day. Holidays or snow can suspend service. | Configure schedules by household/provider, with stream-specific exceptions. |\n| Philadelphia, US | Designated areas have two trash days each week; recycling is only on the first day. The second trash collection is omitted in holiday weeks. | Support multiple weekdays for one stream without duplicating its bin definition. |\n| Berlin, Germany | BSR lists grey residual-waste bins with fortnightly collection. Its collection calendar includes holiday adjustments. | General waste is not necessarily weekly, and colour must be editable. |\n| Paris, France | General waste with green/grey lids is daily; yellow-lid recycling is three days per week; white-lid glass is weekly. Recycling days vary by sector. | Support independent weekday sets, separate glass/paper streams, and daily schedules. |\n| Cambridge/South Cambridgeshire, UK | The council's published winter 2025/26 notice describes green-bin collection changing from fortnightly to monthly, plus individual collections moved earlier or later for holidays. | Seasonal date lists/rules and earlier as well as later exceptions are needed; monthly must not silently mean every four weeks. This is a historical example, not a verified winter 2026/27 schedule. |\n| Amsterdam, Netherlands | Address lookup includes collection days and nearby shared containers for different waste types; bulky waste can be by appointment. | Do not invent a household set-out day for shared/drop-off containers; keep this initial feature focused on scheduled collections. |\n\n### Research sources\n\n- [Seattle: garbage service](https://www.seattle.gov/utilities/your-services/collection-and-disposal/garbage)\n- [Seattle: recycling frequency](https://seattle.gov/utilities/your-services/accounts-and-payments/rates/collection-and-disposal/recycling-rates)\n- [Seattle: service frequencies](https://www.seattle.gov/utilities/construction-resources/collection-and-disposal/storage-and-access)\n- [NYC311: collection schedules](https://portal.311.nyc.gov/article/?kanumber=KA-01801)\n- [Philadelphia: twice-weekly collection areas and holiday exceptions](https://www.phila.gov/2025-12-05-twice-a-week-trash-collection-program-new-initiative-in-the-fight-against-illegal-dumping/)\n- [Berlin BSR: residual-waste bins](https://www.bsr.de/restabfall)\n- [Berlin BSR: collection calendar](https://www.bsr.de/abfuhrkalender)\n- [Paris: collection types, frequencies, lid colours, and sector schedules](https://www.paris.fr/pages/la-collecte-44)\n- [Cambridge: winter 2025/26 frequency changes and holiday exceptions](https://cambridge.gov.uk/news/2025/12/16/changes-to-bin-collections-this-christmas-3)\n- [Amsterdam: address-specific Waste Guide and shared containers](https://www.amsterdam.nl/en/waste-recycling/household-waste/)\n\n## Recommended scheduling model\n\nSeparate **waste stream**, **schedule**, and **display colour**. A stream should have a stable identifier, editable name/colour, and independently configured local collection dates.\n\n- Offer the simple requested preset first, with advanced editing only when needed.\n- For regular schedules, use selected weekdays, an interval in weeks, and a local anchor date for the active week. All seven selected weekdays represents daily collection; multiple selected weekdays covers twice-/three-times-weekly service.\n- Store weekly/fortnightly cycles using anchored local calendar dates, never odd/even ISO week numbers or fixed millisecond durations.\n- Support replace/skip/add exceptions for individual dates without re-anchoring later collections.\n- Consider an explicit-date schedule for irregular or seasonal council calendars. If calendar-month recurrence is offered, specify it separately from an N-week cycle and define short-month behaviour.\n- Group bins by their actual next collection date in the display, even when their schedules differ. An optional set-out note/time is distinct from collection date and from push notifications.\n- A council/suburb/provider name can be an optional label; manual setup must not require sending the household's address to an external lookup service.\n\n## Existing functionality and implementation direction\n\nHomeDash already has calendar sources, source selection and colours, an agenda widget, a fullscreen calendar, and date-only handling for all-day events. Investigate reusing these rather than building a separate calendar or duplicating event rendering.\n\nA dedicated bin-day summary widget may be useful, but whether it is required is an open product decision. Collection schedules must work locally without requiring a council API, cloud service, or internet connection.\n\n## Proposed initial scope\n\n- An administrator can start with the red/yellow/green preset and configure named collection types, colours, and accessible labels.\n- Each collection type has an independent schedule. The requested default is weekly general waste plus alternating fortnightly recycling/garden waste; proposed advanced options cover N-week cycles and multiple weekdays.\n- Show the next collection date and bins due, with clear states such as Today, Tomorrow, and a dated upcoming collection.\n- Represent collection days as local all-day dates; timezone conversion or daylight-saving changes must not move a collection to another day.\n- Support a one-off date change or skipped collection without shifting the underlying recurring schedule.\n- Save schedules persistently and allow existing calendar views to display collection events if the agreed design uses calendar-source integration.\n- Restrict configuration changes to authorised administrators. Anonymous display is permitted only through the existing admin-selected public dashboard and widget visibility rules.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] An administrator can create, edit, and remove a collection schedule, and it persists after reload and restart.\n- [ ] Weekly and fortnightly schedules produce the correct next dates, including two bins due on the same day and alternating recycling weeks.\n- [ ] The default preset uses red general waste every week, yellow recycling on week A, and green garden waste on week B, all on the configured collection weekday.\n- [ ] Every colour and name can be changed without changing collection type identity or recurrence; disabled bins do not appear as due.\n- [ ] If the recommended advanced model is adopted, tests cover twice-weekly trash with once-weekly recycling, daily waste with three recycling weekdays, and independent fortnightly general waste.\n- [ ] Seasonal/explicit-date schedules, if included, preserve the distinction between calendar-month and four-week recurrence and allow exceptions both before and after the original collection day.\n- [ ] A one-off holiday exception changes only the affected occurrence; the next regular collection retains its original cadence.\n- [ ] Today, tomorrow, year boundaries, leap days, and daylight-saving transitions are covered by date-focused tests.\n- [ ] The agreed calendar/summary presentation is readable at 360px and on desktop, in light and dark themes; colour is not the sole indicator of bin type.\n- [ ] Missing configuration has a helpful empty state; loading or schedule errors are explicit rather than presented as no collections.\n- [ ] Auth/authz tests cover permitted administration, rejected unauthorised mutations, and admin-selected public visibility.\n- [ ] Any new persistent schema has migration and database-upgrade coverage; critical setup/display flows have automated tests.\n- [ ] A feature spec and plan, phase logs, related documentation, and the normal Main Version increment accompany implementation.\n\n## Decisions to resolve before implementation\n\n1. Is calendar integration sufficient, or is a dedicated bin-day widget also wanted?\n2. Should schedules apply to one household only, or should multiple properties be supported?\n3. Which household timezone is authoritative for collection dates, and what is the daily rollover behaviour? Do not infer it from a visitor's browser timezone.\n4. Should the first release include the recommended multiple-weekday/N-week and explicit-date options, or deliver the requested preset and manual exceptions first?\n5. Are one-off exceptions entered manually, or should an existing iCal source supply the schedule? Imported dates should be authoritative rather than combined with guessed recurrence.\n6. Should seasonal changes use explicit date lists initially, with richer seasonal/monthly rules deferred?\n\n## Out of initial scope unless explicitly requested\n\nCouncil/address lookup, council scraping, push/email notifications, collection-completion tracking, shared-container service management, and automatic holiday adjustments. Existing calendar providers may still be used, but they must not become a requirement for local scheduling. Do not implement region-wide schedules or colours as authoritative defaults.\n","labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDOUmWVZM8AAAAC3h0j8A","name":"enhancement","description":"New feature or request","color":"a2eeef"},{"id":"LA_kwDOUmWVZM8AAAAC3jj3KQ","name":"widget","description":"Dashboard widget behavior","color":"0E8A16"},{"id":"LA_kwDOUmWVZM8AAAAC3jj3xQ","name":"investigate","description":"Investigation or implementation design required","color":"FBCA04"}],"number":19,"state":"OPEN","title":"Calendar: Track recurring bin collection days","url":"https://github.com/streetratz/HomeDash/issues/19"}
